          Redwood heartwood will not rot just because you never stain it. What it will do, in a season or two of sun, is turn grey, raise its grain and open small checks along the boards. Staining a redwood deck is mostly about keeping the colour and the surface, and that work is won or lost in the prep, not the brushing.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="does-redwood-need-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does Redwood Need to Be Sealed at All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not for survival, usually, but almost always for looks. The red-brown heartwood of redwood contains natural extractives that resist decay and insects, which is why the California Redwood Association recommends heartwood grades for posts, beams and anything on or near the ground. The cream-coloured sapwood found in grades like Deck Common does not share that resistance, so the answer depends partly on which boards you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the grade stamp or your receipt. Deck Heart is an all-heartwood grade with knots, intended for work on or near the ground as well as decking. Deck Common mixes heartwood and sapwood and is rated for above-ground uses such as decking, railings and benches. On a Deck Heart deck, a finish is mainly cosmetic and surface protection. On a Deck Common deck, the sapwood streaks benefit from a finish that keeps water out, and a pigmented stain also helps even out the colour difference between them.
          </p>

          <p>
            What happens to bare redwood is predictable. UV breaks down the surface fibres, the colour shifts toward grey, and repeated wetting and drying raises the grain and opens fine checks. That is not structural damage on heartwood, but restoring it means cleaning, brightening and sometimes sanding before any stain will look even. A deck that is stained early and maintained never needs that rescue.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is an honest third option. If you like the silver-grey look, the CRA describes bleaching or weathering stains that give redwood a uniform grey rather than the patchy grey of untreated wood, and says they need reapplying only if the colour turns uneven. Most people who buy redwood bought it for the red, though, and keeping the red means a pigmented finish and a maintenance routine.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Redwood does not need sealing to last. It needs sealing to stay red.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Heartwood resists rot on its own; UV and water still grey and roughen it. Sapwood-containing boards benefit more from a finish than all-heart boards do.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="new-boards" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sealing a New Redwood Deck: Moisture and Mill Glaze
          </h2>

          <p>
            New redwood decking usually arrives kiln-dried or air-seasoned, so it is closer to stainable than wet pressure-treated pine. The CRA's guidance is that new structures built with unseasoned wood should air-dry for one month before finishing, and that kiln-dried redwood gives the best finish retention. Ready Seal, for its part, asks for a moisture content of 12 percent or less in any species before its stain goes on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture is only half the story. Smooth, planed decking often has mill glaze, a burnished layer created when the planer knives heat the surface. Water and stain bead on it instead of soaking in, and stain applied over it tends to weather off unevenly. The fix is either time, as sun and rain open the surface over a few weeks, or a cleaner that strips the glaze and opens the grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makers disagree on how long to wait, and the reason is useful to understand. The dealer behind the TWP help site tells customers to let new smooth decking weather for four to twelve months before staining, because its product only performs when it fully penetrates. Other makers allow staining much sooner once the wood is clean and dry. Neither is wrong; they are describing different products. Follow the label of the stain you buy, and use the tests below to confirm the wood is ready rather than counting days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two tests settle it. The sprinkle test is free: flick water on several boards in sun and shade, and if it soaks in within a minute or two, the surface is open. If it beads and sits, there is still glaze or too much moisture. A pin moisture meter gives you a number to compare against the label. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-to-wait-to-stain-a-new-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long to wait to stain a new deck</a> walks through both tests in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more point on new decks: TWP's dealer site recommends just one coat on new smooth wood the first time, because the boards cannot yet absorb more, and says to back-wipe whatever has not soaked in after 30 minutes. That principle applies to most penetrating stains. A new redwood deck will take less stain than it will in three years' time, and over-applying it only leaves a sticky film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clean-brighten" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cleaning and Brightening Redwood Before You Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every redwood deck needs cleaning before staining, including brand-new ones. New boards carry mill glaze, construction dirt and handling marks; weathered boards carry grey UV-damaged fibres and mildew. Stain applied over either bonds to a surface that is about to come off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The standard sequence is a cleaner followed by a brightener. The cleaner lifts dirt, grey fibres and mildew. The brightener, usually oxalic acid, neutralises the cleaner, removes extractive and rust staining and brings back redwood's colour. The CRA's own published recipe for restoring weathered or extractive-stained redwood is a scrub with one cup of trisodium phosphate and one cup of household bleach in a gallon of water, followed by four ounces of oxalic acid crystals dissolved in a gallon of warm water, then a thorough rinse once the wood dries. Commercial two-part cleaner and brightener kits follow the same logic in a ready-made form; our <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck cleaner guide</a> covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the right tools. The CRA warns against wire brushes and steel wool, because metal particles can embed in the wood and leave stains; use stiff synthetic bristle brushes instead. A pressure washer can help rinse, but redwood is relatively soft and a narrow tip held close will furrow the grain and raise fibres. Keep the pressure modest and the fan wide, or skip it in favour of a garden hose and a brush. Our article on <a href="/can-you-pressure-wash-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washing a deck</a> covers safe settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanding is the exception, not the rule. If the surface is badly furred or checked, a pass with 60 to 80 grit evens it out; going finer closes the grain and reduces how much stain the wood will take, a point TWP's dealer site makes explicitly. Ready Seal also notes that heavy sanding can draw water-soluble extractives to the surface and cause discolouration, so brighten again after sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then let it dry. Two dry days after washing is a common minimum, longer in shade or humid weather. Retest with water before you open the stain. If you want the reasoning behind the two-step routine, see <a href="/deck-brightener-vs-deck-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck brightener vs deck cleaner</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose-opacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing the Finish: Clear, Semi-Transparent or Solid
          </h2>

          <p>
            The CRA's exterior finishes chart is a good, brand-neutral starting point. Clear water repellents with a mildewcide stabilise redwood at a buckskin tan but can still bleach to grey where sun and rain hit them, and the CRA says reapplication may be needed every 12 to 18 months in humid or harsh climates. On a deck, which takes overhead sun all day, that interval is optimistic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Semi-transparent stains are where most redwood decks end up. They carry enough pigment to slow greying while letting the grain show, and the CRA recommends oil-based versions in this category. When people ask for the best semi-transparent stain for redwood, the products most often in the conversation are penetrating oils with a redwood tone: Ready Seal in Redwood, an oil-based stain and sealer the maker says will not leave laps, runs or streaks; Penofin's redwood product, which the maker describes as giving a rich mahogany-red colour; and TWP 1500 in Redwood, sold as an EPA-registered wood preservative. Our <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best redwood sealer guide</a> compares them properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solid-colour stains are better kept for fences, siding and railings than for deck floors. They form a film, and a film on a walking surface wears through in traffic lanes and then peels at the edges, which turns maintenance into stripping. The CRA goes further for paint and lists it as not recommended for decks and walking or seating surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil or water base is the other choice. Oil-based penetrating stains are the traditional pairing for redwood and are what the CRA recommends for pigmented stains; they soak in, enrich the colour and recoat without stripping. Water-based stains dry faster and clean up with water, but many behave more like a thin film. Our <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">oil vs water-based stain comparison</a> has the full trade-off. Whatever you choose, stay with the same product family for maintenance coats.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
