                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step-by-Step: How to Stain a Redwood Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            1. Pick the window. You need dry weather for the cleaning to dry out and for the stain to soak in. The CRA suggests applying finishes on windless days between 50 and 70 degrees Fahrenheit; your product label may allow more, so follow it. Avoid staining in direct midday sun on a hot day, when the surface can flash-dry before the stain penetrates. Our page on <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what temperature to stain a deck</a> has the details.
          </p>

          <p>
            2. Protect the surroundings. Oil-based penetrating stain soaks into concrete, pavers and stone just as eagerly as into wood. Cover patios, siding and planting beds, and tape the house where the deck meets it. Stir the stain thoroughly and keep stirring during the job so the pigment stays evenly mixed.
          </p>

          <p>
            3. Do the verticals first. Stain railings, balusters, benches and any skirting before the floor, so drips land on unfinished boards you are about to coat anyway. This is the order TWP's dealer site recommends, and it works for any brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            4. Stain the floor two or three boards at a time, full length. Use a stain pad on a pole, a brush, or a pump sprayer followed immediately by a brush or pad to work the stain in. Running each group of boards end to end prevents lap marks where wet and dry stain overlap. Brush stain into the end grain at every butt joint and along the outer edge boards, which take more weather than the middle of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            5. Do not flood it. A penetrating stain should be absorbed. After roughly 30 minutes, wipe or back-brush any stain still sitting shiny on the surface, especially in gaps and under railings where it puddles. Only apply a second coat if the label calls for it and the wood can take it; TWP's dealer recommends wet-on-wet second coats only on wood more than a year old.
          </p>

          <p>
            6. Finish the stairs last and work your way off the deck. Keep people and pets off until the stain is dry to the touch, and leave furniture off for longer. If you are unsure of the general mechanics, our broader <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck staining guide</a> covers tools and technique for any species.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="problem-spots" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Redwood Deck Preservatives, Fasteners and Problem Spots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Redwood has quirks that pine and cedar decks do not, and most of them come from the same extractives that make it durable. Those compounds are water-soluble, so they can leach to the surface and leave dark streaks, especially where water sits or runs. The CRA describes this discolouration and recommends the oxalic acid brightening step above as the remedy. A brightener before staining is the best prevention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fasteners are the second culprit. Ordinary steel nails or screws react with redwood and water to leave black stains around every head. The CRA recommends stainless steel, aluminium or top-quality hot-dipped galvanised fasteners, and if you sand, countersinking galvanised heads first so the coating survives. If you see black rings spreading from the screws on an older deck, replacing those fasteners will do more than any stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a redwood deck preservative rather than just a colour, look for a product registered as one. TWP 1500 Series is sold as an EPA-registered wood preservative for above-ground exterior wood. That matters more on a Deck Common deck with sapwood boards than on all-heart decking. For posts, beams and anything in ground contact, though, the CRA's advice is to use heartwood grades in the first place; a surface treatment brushed onto a finished deck is no substitute for the right lumber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to end grain and cut ends. Every board end and every notch around a post is a path for water. Brush extra stain into those areas, and if you are building, seal cut ends before installation. The CRA recommends coating sawn ends, backs and edges before nailing in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the deck able to dry. Debris packed between boards, planters sitting directly on the decking and soil heaped against the frame all hold moisture against the wood. Clear the gaps once a season. Finishes last longest on boards that dry quickly after rain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance: How Often to Restain a Redwood Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck floors wear through finish much faster than fences or railings. Ready Seal rates its product for one to three years on horizontal surfaces against three to five on vertical surfaces, and recommends a light clean and recoat every two years on a deck. TWP's dealer site suggests a light maintenance coat on the floor in 9 to 24 months. Plan on recoating the floor roughly twice as often as the railings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the wood tell you when. Do the water test each spring: splash water on the high-traffic paths and the sunniest boards. Beading means the finish is still working; water that darkens the wood and soaks in means that area is due. Fading colour on the sunny side is the other signal. You can recoat just the floor, or just the worn lanes, without touching the railings.
          </p>

          <p>
            A maintenance coat is quick if you keep up with it. Wash the deck with a deck cleaner or mild detergent, brighten if it looks dull, let it dry for a couple of days, and apply one light coat of the same product. TWP's dealer recommends re-prepping with a full cleaner and brightener if you go 18 to 24 months between coats. Changing brands is where problems start, because one penetrating oil does not always soak into another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between coats, sweep often and rinse a few times a season. Mildew and dirt hold moisture against the boards and shorten finish life. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-stain-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to stain a deck</a> has a fuller schedule by climate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fences and siding made from the same wood behave very differently, with much longer intervals and their own weak points. If you are finishing a redwood fence as well, read our <a href="/redwood-fence-stain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">redwood fence stain guide</a> before you buy enough stain for both, because the best product for each is not always the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining over mill glaze</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Smooth new redwood decking often has a burnished surface from the planer. Stain beads on it, cures as a thin skin and weathers off unevenly within months. Clean the deck with a wood cleaner or let it weather briefly, then confirm with a sprinkle test that water soaks in before staining.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a wire brush or steel wool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The California Redwood Association warns that metal particles from wire brushes and steel wool embed in the wood and cause stains. On redwood those turn into dark spots under any semi-transparent finish. Use a stiff synthetic bristle brush, and avoid steel fasteners for the same reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting solid stain or paint on the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Film-forming finishes wear through in traffic lanes and then peel, which turns maintenance into stripping. The CRA lists paint as not recommended for decks or walking surfaces. Keep opaque finishes for railings and skirting if you want them, and use a penetrating semi-transparent stain on the boards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-applying on new wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New redwood cannot absorb as much stain as weathered wood. Extra coats do not soak in; they stay on the surface, stay tacky and collect dirt. Apply one coat, wipe or back-brush the excess after about 30 minutes, and save the second coat for later maintenance cycles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating with a different brand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating oils from different makers do not always soak into each other, and some makers say outright that their stain needs bare wood or its own previous coats. If you switch products, plan to strip and brighten first. Otherwise, stay with the same product for every maintenance coat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does redwood need to be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Redwood heartwood resists decay and insects without a finish, so sealing is not essential for it to last. It is needed if you want to keep the colour. Bare redwood turns grey in sun and its surface roughens and checks over time. Sapwood-containing grades such as Deck Common benefit more from a finish than all-heart grades do.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best semi-transparent stain for redwood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An oil-based, penetrating semi-transparent stain in a redwood tone suits most decks; the California Redwood Association recommends oil-based products for pigmented stains. Ready Seal Redwood, Penofin's redwood product and TWP 1500 in Redwood are common choices. Our redwood sealer guide ranks them, but prep matters more than which of the three you pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can you seal a new redwood deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once the boards are dry and the surface is open. The CRA suggests a month of air-drying for unseasoned wood, and Ready Seal asks for 12 percent moisture or less. Some makers, such as TWP's dealer, recommend waiting several months on smooth decking. Clean off mill glaze, then use a sprinkle test or moisture meter rather than a calendar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a redwood deck sealer or a stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A clear redwood deck sealer keeps water out but does little to stop UV greying, so on a sunny deck it needs reapplying often. A semi-transparent stain adds pigment that slows greying and evens out colour between heartwood and sapwood. If you want the deck to stay red, choose a tinted stain; clear is for shaded decks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best redwood deck preservative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you want preservative protection rather than colour alone, look for a product registered as a wood preservative, such as TWP 1500 Series, which is sold as EPA-registered for above-ground exterior wood. It matters most on decks with sapwood boards. For posts and ground contact, use heartwood grades; a surface coating cannot make sapwood suitable for soil.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of stain does a redwood deck need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually one on new smooth boards and up to two on weathered or well-absorbing wood, depending on the product. The CRA says two coats are usually required for semi-transparent stains on new wood, while TWP's dealer recommends one coat on new decking. The rule that never changes: wipe off whatever has not soaked in after about 30 minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use cedar-tone stain on redwood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Any penetrating exterior wood stain that suits cedar will generally suit redwood; the two species behave similarly. The difference is colour. Redwood is redder to begin with, so a cedar tone will look warmer on it than on the sample card. Test on an offcut or a hidden board, after brightening, before committing to a full deck.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

