        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Staining a redwood deck is not difficult, but the order matters. Confirm the boards are dry and the glaze is gone, clean and brighten, choose a penetrating semi-transparent stain in a redwood tone, apply it thinly with verticals first, and wipe off what does not soak in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            After that, the deck asks for a wash each spring and a light recoat every couple of years. Keep that rhythm and you will never have to strip it. For product choices, our redwood sealer rankings are the next stop.
          </p>
